📗
  🗝 Names and the places they hold. The first name added holds place 0, the
  next place 1, and a name keeps its place for good. Whoever wants a value
  for each name keeps the values in a list of its own, by place; 📓 does.

  Up to eight names are looked through one by one. Beyond that they are found
  through an index, hashed with a key that is drawn anew by every process
  (🧂🐇🔩). A client that picks the names, as it does in a JSON object, can
  then not pick ones that all fall into the same slot of the index and turn
  every lookup into a walk past all the others.

  This is used wherever the standard library's dictionary would be: that one
  leaks an entry whenever two keys share a bucket.
